    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d767">

                <pb facs="1049" n="1039" sameAs="frus:frus1946v10/pg1039"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">Marshall Mission Files, Lot 54–D270:
                    Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Thomas B. McCabe to Mr. John K. Howard, at
                        Manila</hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1946-06-15">15 June 1946</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>91468. I am unofficially advising you that you are authorized to increase the
                    Chinese credit from outstanding amount of $250,000,000 <pb facs="1050" n="1040"
                        xml:id="pg1040"/> to a total of $425,000,000<anchor n="21" xml:id="d767-f1"
                    /> which you indicated. The Chinese National Debt<anchor n="22" xml:id="d767-f2"
                    /> of approximately $80,000,000 which I understand remains at this time unused
                    is temporarily withdrawn for use in connection with a proposed bulk sale to
                    China. I suggest you go ahead with your negotiations until such time as I can
                    restore either all or some part of the remaining Chinese National Debt for your
                    purpose. Keep me advised.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d767-f1" n="21" xml:id="d767-f1-content">
                <p>Formal approval to grant the Chinese Government a credit up to $425,000,000 for
                    the purchase of all uncommitted surplus in the Pacific was given by Assistant
                    Secretary of State William L. Clayton in his memorandum of June 18 to Mr. McCabe
                    (893.24FLC/6–1846).</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d767-f2" n="22" xml:id="d767-f2-content">
                <p>The debt due China for advances of Chinese national currency to American military
                    forces in China.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
